5 Quick Wins to Slash Your AWS Bill!!!!!

Want to Cut Your AWS Costs? Here Are 5 Simple (But Powerful) Tips.

Cloud bills can sneak up on you if you're not careful. If you're using AWS, here’s how you can take control of your cloud spend without compromising performance:
1. Know Your Spend
The first step to reducing costs is visibility. Just like checking your mobile banking app, use AWS Cost Explorer to get a clear view of where your money is going.
Filter, group, and analyze daily/monthly trends.
 For deeper insights, explore the Cost and Usage Reports or Cloud Intelligence Dashboards to pinpoint resource-level usage.

2️. Turn Things Off
Leaving EC2 or RDS instances running when not in use? That's like leaving your lights on 24/7.
 Use AWS Instance Scheduler to automatically shut down non-production resources during nights or weekends.
 Tag resources, set the schedule, and save up to 50% it's that simple.

3️. Choose Graviton by Default
AWS Graviton processors offer better price-performance for many workloads.
 If you're using managed services like RDS, EMR, or OpenSearch, switch to Graviton-based instances without rewriting your code.
 Pro Tip: Make Graviton your default in Infrastructure as Code (IaC) for instant, ongoing savings.

4️.  Don’t Forget the “Little” Services
Services like CloudWatch, EKS, EBS, Elastic IPs, and Load Balancers often go unnoticed and that’s where surprise charges come from.
 Use Cost Explorer to monitor them.
 Use AWS Compute Optimizer to right-size and detach unused resources.
 For CloudWatch, keep only necessary logs to avoid bloated storage and processing costs.

5️. Set Spend Alerts
Be proactive.
Use AWS Budgets to get alerts as you approach your monthly limits.
Use Anomaly Detection to catch unexpected spikes in services like CloudWatch, even if everything else looks “normal.”
Pro Tip: All new AWS accounts have anomaly detection turned on by default make sure you’re using it!
